9\n10.293\n9.450\n14.330\n4.838\nPlum Island Estuary - West Creek\n\n\n2016-08-13\n42.7\nPIE\n9.92\n10.293\n9.450\n14.330\n4.838\nPlum Island Estuary - West Creek\n\n\n2016-08-13\n42.7\nPIE\n16.18\n10.293\n9.450\n14.330\n4.838\nPlum Island Estuary - West Creek\n\n\n2016-08-13\n42.7\nPIE\n18.06\n10.293\n9.450\n14.330\n4.838\nPlum Island Estuary - West Creek\n\n\n2016-08-13\n42.7\nPIE\n19.37\n10.293\n9.450\n14.330\n4.838\nPlum Island Estuary - West Creek\n\n\n2016-08-13\n42.7\nPIE\n17.52\n10.293\n9.450\n14.330\n4.838\nPlum Island Estuary - West Creek\n\n\n2016-08-13\n42.7\nPIE\n17.71\n10.293\n9.450\n14.330\n4.838\nPlum Island Estuary - West Creek\n\n\n2016-08-13\n42.7\nPIE\n20.42\n10.293\n9.450\n14.330\n4.838\nPlum Island Estuary - West Creek\n\n\n2016-08-13\n42.7\nPIE\n20.24\n10.293\n9.450\n14.330\n4.838\nPlum Island Estuary - West Creek\n\n\n2016-08-13\n42.7\nPIE\n18.67\n10.293\n9.450\n14.330\n4.838\nPlum Island Estuary - West Creek\n\n\n2016-08-13\n42.7\nPIE\n17.92\n10.293\n9.450\n14.330\n4.838\nPlum Island Estuary - West Creek\n\n\n2016-08-13\n42.7\nPIE\n21.20\n10.293\n9.450\n14.330\n4.838\nPlum Island Estuary - West Creek\n\n\n2016-08-13\n42.7\nPIE\n16.25\n10.293\n9.450\n14.330\n4.838\nPlum Island Estuary - West Creek\n\n\n2016-08-13\n42.7\nPIE\n20.32\n10.293\n9.450\n14.330\n4.838\nPlum Island Estuary - West Creek\n\n\n2016-08-13\n42.7\nPIE\n18.05\n10.293\n9.450\n14.330\n4.838\nPlum Island Estuary - West Creek\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThis data set comes with lots of columns! Let’s see how we would be able to narrow it down. We can use the select function to create a new data set that only has the desired columns. Test it out below! Pay attention to how the column names inside the select() functions changes as you change what columns to include. Note that only 15 rows are shown, but the data is R actually has 392!\n\nSelect function\n\nviewof col1 = Inputs.select(col_options, {label: \"Column 1\"})\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nviewof col2 = Inputs.select(col_options, {label: \"Column 2\"})\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nviewof col3 = Inputs.select(col_options, {label: \"Column 3\"})\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nrows = transpose(choices)\nmatch = rows.find(d =&gt; d.col1 === col1 && d.col2 === col2 && d.col3 === col3)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n{\n  const pre = document.createElement(\"pre\");\n  pre.className = \"plot-code\";\n  const code = document.createElement(\"code\");\n  code.textContent = match.code;\n  pre.appendChild(code);\n  return pre;\n}\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n{\n  const wrap = document.createElement(\"div\");\n  wrap.className = \"side-by-side\";\n  wrap.innerHTML =\n    \"&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Before&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\" + match.before + \"&lt;/div&gt;\" +\n    \"&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;After&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\" + match.after + \"&lt;/div&gt;\";\n  return wrap;\n}\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nFilter function\nAnother important function from the dplyr package is filter(). This function only keeps rows that meet certain conditions. Try it out below. Again, we are only looking at 15 rows of the 392 that the whole data set has. Pay attention to how sites and carapace size are excluded based on the conditions specified in the code.\n\nviewof site_filt = Inputs.select(site_options_filt, {label: \"Site\"})\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nviewof min_size_filt = Inputs.select(size_options_filt, {label: \"Minimum size\"})\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nrows_filt = transpose(choices_filt)\nmatch_filt = rows_filt.find(d =&gt; d.site === site_filt && d.size === min_size_filt)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n{\n  const pre = document.createElement(\"pre\");\n  pre.className = \"plot-code\";\n  const code = document.createElement(\"code\");\n  code.textContent = match_filt.code;\n  pre.appendChild(code);\n  return pre;\n}\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n{\n  const wrap = document.createElement(\"div\");\n  wrap.className = \"side-by-side\";\n  wrap.innerHTML =\n    \"&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Before&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\" + match_filt.before + \"&lt;/div&gt;\" +\n    \"&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;After&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\" + match_filt.after + \"&lt;/div&gt;\";\n  return wrap;\n}\n\n\n\n\n\n\nFor this analysis, we are going to keep all the crabs, so there is no need to filter our data. But, in other situations, it may be helpful to look at trends within one specific site, so the filter function would come in handy.\n\n\nSummarise function\nTo better understand the summarise() function, we will filter the data to a specific site and calculate some summary statistics about that site.\n\nviewof site_fsum = Inputs.select(site_options_fsum, {label: \"Site\"})\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nviewof stats_fsum = Inputs.checkbox(summary_options_fsum, {label: \"Summaries\"})\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nrows_fsum = transpose(choices_fsum)\ntables_fsum = transpose(site_tables)\nmatch_fsum = rows_fsum.find(d =&gt; d.site === site_fsum && d.stats === stats_fsum.join(\"|\"))\nbefore_fsum = tables_fsum.find(d =&gt; d.site === site_fsum)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n{\n  const pre = document.createElement(\"pre\");\n  pre.className = \"plot-code\";\n  const code = document.createElement(\"code\");\n  code.textContent = match_fsum.code;\n  pre.appendChild(code);\n  return pre;\n}\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n{\n  const wrap = document.createElement(\"div\");\n  wrap.className = \"side-by-side\";\n  wrap.innerHTML =\n    \"&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Before:&lt;/b&gt; filtered rows (first 10 of \" + before_fsum.n + \")&lt;/p&gt;\" + before_fsum.before + \"&lt;/div&gt;\" +\n    \"&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;After&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\" + match_fsum.after + \"&lt;/div&gt;\";\n  return wrap;\n}\n\n\n\n\n\n\nNow let’s make a new data set that just has some summary statistics of carapace size. We will utilise the group_by() and the summarise() (or summarize()) functions to do this. Since these samples are taken from multiple sites across a latitudinal gradient, the group by function will help us keep track of the different sites. Test out what happens when you group by site versus when you don’t!\n\nviewof group_gsum = Inputs.select(group_options_gsum, {label: \"Group by\"})\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nviewof stats_gsum = Inputs.checkbox(summary_options_gsum, {label: \"Summaries\"})\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nrows_gsum = transpose(choices_gsum)\nmatch_gsum = rows_gsum.find(d =&gt; d.group === group_gsum && d.stats === stats_gsum.join(\"|\"))\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n{\n  const pre = document.createElement(\"pre\");\n  pre.className = \"plot-code\";\n  const code = document.createElement(\"code\");\n  code.textContent = match_gsum.code;\n  pre.appendChild(code);\n  return pre;\n}\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n{\n  const div = document.createElement(\"div\");\n  div.innerHTML = match_gsum.table;\n  return div;\n}\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nPlotting Our Data\nOne important step in data exploration is visualization. We can use plots to get an understanding of how our variables are related to each other.\ngeom_point() draws one dot per row of your data. Choose which columns go on each axis and optionally colour the dots by a third column. See what relationships you can find between different variables.\nI am going to use the select() and summarise() functions to help clean the data to create a figure using ggplot2.\n\ncrab_summary &lt;- crab_data %&gt;% \n  select(site, size, latitude) %&gt;% \n  group_by(site) %&gt;% \n  summarise(mean_size = mean(size), \n            latitude = unique(latitude),\n            sd_size = sd(size))\n\n\n\n\n\n\nFiddler crab carapace size (mm) in relation to latitude. Light grey points represent individual observations, while black larger points show mean values for sample sites. Smooth shows positive relationship between carapace size and latitude.\n\n\n\n\nSee if you can create the code by changing the options with the dropdowns.\n\nviewof geom_sel = Inputs.select(geom_options, {label: \"Geom\"})\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nviewof smooth_sel = Inputs.select(smooth_options, {label: \"Smooths\"})\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nviewof theme_sel = Inputs.select(theme_options, {label: \"Themes\"})\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nrows_plot = transpose(plot_choices)\nmatch_plot = rows_plot.find(d =&gt; \nd.data_geom === geom_sel && d.smooths === smooth_sel && d.themes === theme_sel)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n{\nconst img = document.createElement(\"img\");\nimg.src = match_plot.image;\nimg.style.maxWidth = \"100%\";\nreturn img;\n}\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe code that made this plot:\n\n{\nconst pre = document.createElement(\"pre\");\npre.className = \"plot-code\";\nconst code = document.createElement(\"code\");\ncode.textContent = match_plot ? match_plot.code : \"\";\npre.appendChild(code);\nreturn pre;\n}"
